Z||000000||Richard Parnell||05-08-2000||07:29 PM||||Can't seem to get an answer on the tech ques side.  Does anyone one where I can order a slave cylinder for the hydraulic clutch on my 87 coupe 4 cyl 5 speed?  I need the long tubular one not the short cast one.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||05-08-2000||08:13 PM||||Hi there!<P>I got an 87 GT but I don't think the slave cylinder would be different.  In anyevent I had to replace mine and was done by RSM their phone number is 416-524-4285 and the email address is <A HREF="http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/." TARGET=_blank>http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/.</A>   I'm sure they are able to help you and even send it out for you via mail if you're not around the S Ontario region.  Hope this helps..<P>Later<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Richard Parnell:<BR><B>Can't seem to get an answer on the tech ques side.  Does anyone one where I can order a slave cylinder for the hydraulic clutch on my 87 coupe 4 cyl 5 speed?  I need the long tubular one not the short cast one.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||05-08-2000||11:36 PM||||The 4-cyl. & V-6 slave cylinders are different (different bores, etc).  You need to order a new GM slave from a GM dealer....don't waste your time or $$ on ANY aftermarket slave.  It just isn't worth the hassles in the long run.  I went through 3 aftermarket slaves on an '88 Formula within an 8 month span.....all of them were junk.  I finally spent the extra money on a new GM slave and all was well.    The initial cost is higher, but it more than pays for itself when you bleed the system (the aftermarket ones seem to be unbleedable).<P>Good luck. ||||reg||1
